00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.400
The musical's set design is also packed with&nbsp;
lots of details that reflect both the pre-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.680
and post-revolutionary era of the United States.
As Hamilton's Creative Director and Designer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.280
David Korins has explained,
"this is the story of the people who built the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.080
scaffolding upon which the country was built,
so you see wooden period scaffolding up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.920
around a half-made wall to show&nbsp;
a kind of aspirational space,"
&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.440
giving it the feel of a "Colonial&nbsp;
era building under construction" 
&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.040
And during the musical's intermission, the&nbsp;
brick walls get bigger, growing 8 feet taller
&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.400
"to represent the continuous work being done on&nbsp;
the country" and how the foundation is growing.
&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.840
By the way, fun fact, those&nbsp;
brick walls aren't brick at all,
&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.320
but a "pressed pattern board that is then&nbsp;
cut up and treated with goop and texture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.720
paint, and layers of distressing on top."
&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.920
Korins has also said that the use&nbsp;
of turntables on the stage is
&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.360
"inspired by the whirlwind of&nbsp;
history that sweeps up Hamilton,
&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.680
as well as the literal hurricane that hits&nbsp;
the Caribbean island where he was born."
&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.080
And it also matches the "cyclical&nbsp;
relationship between Aaron Burr and Hamilton,
&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.200
where they were basically spiralling around&nbsp;
each other their entire careers and lives."
&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.400
Like the line "Immigrants. We get the job done",
which always gets huge applause in the theatre,
&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.840
the show's set design also has its&nbsp;
own way of honouring the immigrants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.720
that helped build America.
Korins has said that since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.320
many immigrants arrived via boat,
they wanted to feature nautical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.560
elements in their set designs which is why&nbsp;
there's lots of ropes and pulleys and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:17.440
wooden structures are built using the same&nbsp;
elements used in shipbuilding of the era.
&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.800
The show's choreography also plays a crucial&nbsp;
part in creating subtle effects to complement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.640
the story at any particular time.
For example, when Hamilton arrives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.280
in "Helpless", both Angelica and Eliza&nbsp;
are positioned along his line of sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.240
and in this moment he locks eyes with Eliza.
When the moment is rewound in "Satisfied",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.480
Hamilton's position switches over, and&nbsp;
this time he's looking at Angelica.
&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.280
The direction in which the turntable&nbsp;
moves also communicates a subtle meaning.
&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.360
For choreographer Andy Blankenbuehler,&nbsp;
counter-clockwise motion like that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.360
"Helpless" suggests time passing.
The opposite direction, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.160
implies there is "resistance to the inevitable"&nbsp;
going on, which is why the turntable reverses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.640
its motion during "Satisfied".
Blankenbuehler explains that many in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.280
audience won't "consciously register his choices
like the fact that Burr moves in straight lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.480
because he sees no options,
and Hamilton moves in arcs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.120
because he sees all possibilities",
but that viewers still feel it intuitively.
